>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_codatatype`

``` hol4
Refute.register_codatatype :
  {tyop : {Thy : string, Tyop : string},
   case_const : term,
   constructors : term list,
   witness : thm option} -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Registers an existing codatatype with the model finder.

`tyop` names the codatatype operator, `constructors` gives its distinct
constructor constants in cases-theorem order, and `case_const` is its
case constant with a unique valid scrutinee position (in any argument,
not necessarily the first).  Refute validates and normalizes polymorphic
constructor types, infers that scrutinee position, and checks each
remaining argument against the corresponding constructor branch.
Type-operator parameters must be distinct type variables.

Every constructor must declare - at its own declared type, not merely at
whatever instance the caller applied it - a `tyop`-headed result type;
the shape check rejects a constant such as `combin$I`, whose declared
result type is a bare type variable and would otherwise match any
`tyop` at a suitable instance.  When `tyop` is a type the datatype
database already knows as a free datatype, every registered constructor
must also be one of that type's database constructors, by key; this
rejects an impostor constant declared at the right type without needing
a witness.  For a type the database does not know, this imposes
nothing, and constructor/case shape alone cannot separate an inductive
datatype from a codatatype, so `witness = NONE` registers on trust: a
wrong registration removes the type's acyclicity constraints and can
produce a wrong model.  The built-in registrations below are trusted
this same way too, but through a different path: their lazy harvest
calls the shape check directly rather than `register_codatatype`, so no
witness ever applies to them.  `witness = SOME thm` narrows what a
caller can get away with further: after stripping `thm`'s outer `!`s,
its conclusion must be `?x. body`, `body` an equation with one side
`aconv` to `x` (either orientation) and the other a constructor
application `C a1 ... an`, where `C`'s key matches a registered
constructor and `x` is `aconv` to some argument reached by walking `C`'s
constructor spine - descending into an argument only when its own head
is again a registered constructor - not merely free somewhere in it.
Every registered constructor's *declared* result type is `tyop`-headed,
so `C`'s key match makes it literally that declared constant; `C` need
not be fully applied, so `x`'s own type need not be `tyop`-headed, and
`x`'s type need not be the generic instance either.  HOL4's per-type
bisimulation theorems and mutual-cycle witnesses are not of this shape
and are not accepted; a caller holding one passes `witness = NONE`.
Together, the witness rules out generic shape defeats and the database
cross-check rules out impostor constants for types the database knows;
beyond that, the constructor list remains the caller's assertion.

Registrations are session-local.  Built-in lazy registrations cover
`llist`, `ltree`, `itree`, `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when their
theories are available.

### Failure

Fails for an empty or duplicate constructor list, nonconstant terms,
wrong constructor results or case branches, ambiguous or absent
scrutinee position, interpreted or function types, malformed type
parameters, a witness that does not have the accepted shape, or an
incompatible quotient, typedef, or fraction classification.

### See also

[`Refute.register_quotient`](#Refute.register_quotient),
[`Refute.register_typedef`](#Refute.register_typedef),
[`Refute.register_frac_type`](#Refute.register_frac_type),
[`Refute.model_refute`](#Refute.model_refute)

Codatatype bounds are described in \ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
